st sits down with johnston  and john breckenridge who was a former  vice president  and um and in breckenridge would join  him for the negotiations and at one  point  um the confederates will be laying down  such terms and and  sherman will move his chair back and say  well see here who's who's  surrendering to whom and um but in fact  sherman will give very generous terms  to the confederates and in doing so he  thinks he's carrying out lincoln's  vision  but he will be sharply rebuked by the by  the union cabinet particularly after the  death of of lincoln the assassination  and grant will actually be sent down to  talk to sherman  now this is a fascinating scene too  because in the union capital when this  happens  the attorney general james speed will  say well what if sherman decides to  arrest grant  and speed and stanton were convinced  that that sherman was potentially  thinking of some kind of a coup  and as they put it he was getting ready  to march northward with his allegiance  to take over  that's the kind of chaos and anarchy  that was gripping the north post  lincoln assassination while there's  still confederate armies in the field  so if johnson had decided to go to the  hills and along with lee to  sanction some kind of real warfare we  would have been in a real mess but  johnson instead followed lee's example  and and um and he too would surrender as  we said earlier in this act of basic  basically insubordination and in doing  so it would really  pave the way for this country to become  not like the balkans not like the middle  east  uh not like um webbing in our cambodia  or these other civil war torn countries  or northern ireland  but to become like america today so was  john wilkes booth working on his own  yeah that's a it's a great question and  it's one i don't know the answer to  very few issues did i duck in my own  mind when i went into this book whenever  i went into an issue i wanted to resolve  it in its entirety  that was something 